    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 3.1 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-optimum-by-sampling">
        <title>Finding the Optimum by Sampling</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> the optimal solution of a linear programming problem is at a vertex of the feasible region; a vertex that is not a whole-number point is not an answer when the unknowns count things.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> find the vertices of R, work out the objective function at each, and sample the whole-number points of R near the best vertex.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> choose the most profitable plan for a fundraiser, a tailor and a youth group, and the cheapest fertiliser for a shamba.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners test points of the fundraiser region in the profit function and notice that the best plans are at a corner.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Give each group two inside points and two corners to test, and collect every result in one table on the board. After part (c), ask: <q>Could any plan inside R beat the best corner?</q></p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-which-plan-earns-most">
            <title>Which Plan Earns Most?</title>
            <statement>
                <p>The Grade 12 class in Machakos is planning its fundraiser again (<xref ref="act-planning-the-fundraiser"/>). They make <m>x</m> mandazi and <m>y</m> samosas, with
                    <md>x + y \le 100, \quad 15x + 35y \le 2520, \quad y \ge 30, \quad x \ge 0</md>.
                    The profit is Ksh <m>15</m> on a mandazi and Ksh <m>25</m> on a samosa, so the profit in shillings is <m>P = 15x + 25y</m>. The corners of the region R are <m>(0, 30)</m>, <m>(70, 30)</m>, <m>(49, 51)</m> and <m>(0, 72)</m> (<xref ref="act-corners-of-the-fundraiser-region"/>).</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Check that the plans <m>(20, 40)</m>, <m>(50, 40)</m> and <m>(30, 50)</m> are inside R, and work out the profit for each.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>All three satisfy every constraint. <m>P = 1300</m>, <m>1750</m> and <m>1700</m> shillings.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Work out the profit at each corner of R.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>(0, 30)</m>: Ksh <m>750</m>; <m>(70, 30)</m>: Ksh <m>1800</m>; <m>(49, 51)</m>: Ksh <m>2010</m>; <m>(0, 72)</m>: Ksh <m>1800</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Which plan earns most? How much is the profit?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>49 mandazi and 51 samosas, a profit of Ksh <m>2010</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Start at the plan <m>(50, 40)</m>. What happens to the profit if the class makes one more samosa? Can they keep doing this? Use your answer to explain why the best plan is on the edge of R, and then at a corner.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>One more samosa adds Ksh <m>25</m>. From any point inside R you can keep adding samosas (or mandazi) and the profit keeps rising until you reach a boundary. Along that boundary you can still move in a direction that raises the profit until you reach a corner, where two limits stop you at once.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To state where the optimum of a linear objective lies, and the sampling method for whole-number answers.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> no inside plan in the board table beat the best corner. That is the key fact: the optimum is at a vertex of R.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Sampling: find the vertices, work out the objective at each, choose the best.</p></li>
                    <li><p>When the answer must be whole numbers and the best vertex is not a whole-number point, test the whole-number points of R near it, check the one you choose against every constraint, and check that no whole-number point further away does better.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-optimum-by-sampling">
            <title>Optimal Solution by Sampling</title>
            <p>The point of R that gives the largest value of the objective function (or the smallest, for a cost) is the <term>optimal solution</term>. The value of the objective function there is the <term>optimum value</term>.</p>
            <p>For a linear objective function such as <m>P = ax + by</m>, the optimum value is found at a vertex of R: the best value of a linear objective is at a corner of the region. If the lines <m>ax + by = k</m> are parallel to an edge of R, every point of that edge may give the same optimum value.</p>
            <p>To find the optimal solution by <term>sampling</term>:
                <ol marker="1.">
                    <li><p>Find the vertices of R.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Work out the objective function at each vertex.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Choose the vertex with the largest value (or the smallest, for a cost).</p></li>
                    <li><p>If the answer must be whole numbers and the best vertex is not a whole-number point, list the whole-number points of R near that vertex (a good start is every whole-number point whose <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are each within <m>2</m> of it) and work out the objective at each. Choose the best and check that it satisfies every constraint.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Check that no whole-number point of R further from the vertex does better.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
            <p>Step 4 is a practical method, not a rule that always works: the best whole-number point is usually near the best vertex, but in a long, thin region it can lie further away. That is why step 5 is needed. When R is small, as in every problem in this chapter, the nearby points are enough, and you can check by working out the objective at the other whole-number points of R. A vertex that is not a whole-number point is not an answer. Rounding a vertex can carry you outside R, so check every constraint.</p>
            <p xml:id="p-search-line-ahead-core" component="core">In the next lesson you meet a second method, the search line, which also gives a quick check for step 5.</p>
            <p xml:id="p-sampling-only-essential" component="essential">Sampling is the method you will use for every problem in this book.</p>
        </insight>

        <figure xml:id="fig-lp-sampling-points">
            <caption>Drag the point to a whole-number plan for the tailor of <xref ref="act-the-tailors-week"/>. If it is in R you see its profit; try to beat the best so far. Then show the vertices.</caption>
            <interactive xml:id="interactive-lp-sampling-points" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="16:12" source="jsxgraph/lp-sampling-points.js">
                <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-lp-sampling-points" surface="jsxboard" aspect="16:12"/>
                <instructions>
                    <p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><p>The tailor makes <m>x</m> shirts and <m>y</m> dresses a week, with <m>3x + 7y \le 126</m> (cloth) and <m>x + 6y \le 64</m> (machine hours). The profit is <m>P = 300x + 800y</m> shillings. The unwanted side of each line is shaded; R is left clear.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Drag the point <alert>Q</alert>. It jumps to whole-number plans. The text says whether the plan is in R and, if it is, its profit.</p></li>
                            <li><p>The best plan you have found so far is marked and recorded. Can you reach Ksh <m>13\,200</m>?</p></li>
                            <li><p>Press <alert>Show the vertices</alert> to see the four corners of R with their profits. Is your best plan at a corner?</p></li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </instructions>
            </interactive>
        </figure>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-tailor-best-week">
            <title>The Tailor's Best Week</title>
            <statement>
                <p>The tailor in Kisumu makes <m>x</m> school shirts and <m>y</m> dresses a week, with
                    <md>3x + 7y \le 126, \quad x + 6y \le 64, \quad x \ge 0, \quad y \ge 0</md>
                    and profit <m>P = 300x + 800y</m> shillings (<xref ref="act-the-tailors-week"/>). The vertices of R are <m>(0, 0)</m>, <m>(42, 0)</m>, <m>(28, 6)</m> and <m>\left(0, \tfrac{32}{3}\right)</m> (<xref ref="example-tailor-vertices"/>). How many of each should the tailor make to earn the most?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Work out <m>P</m> at each vertex.</p>
                <table xml:id="table-tailor-vertex-profits">
                    <title>Profit at the Vertices of the Tailor's Region</title>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Vertex</cell><cell><m>P = 300x + 800y</m> (Ksh)</cell></row>
                        <row><cell><m>(0, 0)</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell></row>
                        <row><cell><m>(42, 0)</m></cell><cell><m>12\,600</m></cell></row>
                        <row><cell><m>(28, 6)</m></cell><cell><m>8400 + 4800 = 13\,200</m></cell></row>
                        <row><cell><m>\left(0, \tfrac{32}{3}\right)</m></cell><cell><m>8533.33</m></cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <p>The largest value is at <m>(28, 6)</m>, which is already a whole-number point. The tailor should make <m>28</m> shirts and <m>6</m> dresses a week, for a profit of Ksh <m>13\,200</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-cheapest-fertiliser">
            <title>Cheapest Fertiliser</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Moraa buys fertiliser for her shamba in Kisii. A bag of fertiliser A costs Ksh <m>4000</m> and a bag of fertiliser B costs Ksh <m>7000</m>. She needs at least <m>7</m> bags altogether. A bag of A gives <m>1</m> unit of nitrogen and a bag of B gives <m>6</m> units; the shamba needs at least <m>17</m> units. She buys <m>x</m> bags of A and <m>y</m> bags of B. Find the cheapest way to buy the fertiliser.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>The constraints are <m>x + y \ge 7</m>, <m>x + 6y \ge 17</m>, <m>x \ge 0</m>, <m>y \ge 0</m>, and the cost is <m>C = 4000x + 7000y</m> shillings.</p>
                <p>Solve <m>x + y = 7</m> and <m>x + 6y = 17</m> together: subtracting gives <m>5y = 10</m>, so <m>y = 2</m> and <m>x = 5</m>. The other vertices are where the boundaries meet the axes: <m>x + 6y = 17</m> meets <m>y = 0</m> at <m>(17, 0)</m>, and <m>x + y = 7</m> meets <m>x = 0</m> at <m>(0, 7)</m>.</p>
                <p><md>
                    <mrow>(17, 0)\amp: \quad C = 68\,000</mrow>
                    <mrow>(5, 2)\amp: \quad C = 20\,000 + 14\,000 = 34\,000</mrow>
                    <mrow>(0, 7)\amp: \quad C = 49\,000</mrow>
                </md>
                    The cheapest plan is <m>5</m> bags of A and <m>2</m> bags of B, costing Ksh <m>34\,000</m>.</p>
                <p>Here R has no far edge: it carries on away from the origin, and buying more bags only costs more. The minimum is still at a vertex.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-beehives-and-cages">
            <title>Beehives and Cages</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A youth group in Kitui makes <m>x</m> beehives and <m>y</m> chicken cages a month. A beehive uses <m>7</m> planks and a cage <m>3</m> planks; the group has <m>35</m> planks. A beehive needs <m>3</m> hours on the group's saw and a cage <m>7</m> hours; the saw is free for <m>31</m> hours. The profit is Ksh <m>900</m> on a beehive and Ksh <m>700</m> on a cage. How many of each should the group make?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>The model is <m>7x + 3y \le 35</m>, <m>3x + 7y \le 31</m>, <m>x \ge 0</m>, <m>y \ge 0</m>, with <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> whole numbers, and <m>P = 900x + 700y</m>.</p>
                <p><alert>Step 1: the vertices.</alert> Solve <m>7x + 3y = 35</m> and <m>3x + 7y = 31</m>: multiply the first by <m>7</m> and the second by <m>3</m> and subtract, <m>40x = 152</m>, so <m>x = 3.8</m> and <m>y = 2.8</m>. The vertices are <m>(0, 0)</m>, <m>(5, 0)</m>, <m>(3.8, 2.8)</m> and <m>\left(0, \tfrac{31}{7}\right) \approx (0, 4.43)</m>.</p>
                <p><alert>Step 2: the objective at each vertex.</alert> <m>P = 0</m>, <m>4500</m>, <m>5380</m> and <m>3100</m>. The best vertex is <m>(3.8, 2.8)</m>, but the group cannot make <m>3.8</m> beehives.</p>
                <p><alert>Step 3: do not just round.</alert> Rounding gives <m>(4, 3)</m>, which needs <m>7 \times 4 + 3 \times 3 = 37</m> planks: more than <m>35</m>, so <m>(4, 3)</m> is outside R.</p>
                <p><alert>Step 4: sample the whole-number points near the vertex.</alert> Take <m>x = 2, 3, 4, 5</m> and <m>y = 1, 2, 3, 4</m>. The points of this block that are in R, with their profits, are
                    <md>(2, 1)\colon 2500, \quad (2, 2)\colon 3200, \quad (2, 3)\colon 3900, \quad (3, 1)\colon 3400,</md>
                    <md>(3, 2)\colon 4100, \quad (3, 3)\colon 4800, \quad (4, 1)\colon 4300, \quad (4, 2)\colon 5000</md>.
                    The others break a constraint (for example <m>(5, 1)</m> needs <m>38</m> planks). The vertex <m>(5, 0)</m> gives only <m>4500</m>.</p>
                <p><alert>Step 5: check.</alert> <m>(4, 2)</m> uses <m>28 + 6 = 34</m> planks and <m>12 + 14 = 26</m> saw hours, so it is in R. The region is small, so <xref ref="fig-beehives-region"/> shows all <m>22</m> of its whole-number points: none beats <m>(4, 2)</m>. The group should make <m>4</m> beehives and <m>2</m> cages, for a profit of Ksh <m>5000</m>.</p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-beehives-region">
                    <caption>The youth group's region R with its whole-number points. The best vertex <m>(3.8, 2.8)</m> is a ring; the rounded point <m>(4, 3)</m>, a cross, lies outside R.</caption>
                    <image width="70%">
                        <shortdescription>Region for beehives x and cages y. Solid lines 7x + 3y = 35 and 3x + 7y = 31; the side of each away from the origin is shaded. R, left clear, has corners (0, 0), (5, 0), (3.8, 2.8) and (0, 4.43). Dots mark its 22 whole-number points; the best, (4, 2), is labelled. A cross at (4, 3) lies in the shaded part.</shortdescription>
                        <description><p>Horizontal axis x, beehives, from 0 to 7. Vertical axis y, cages, from 0 to 6. Boundary 7x + 3y = 35, solid, through (5, 0) and (2.43, 6). Boundary 3x + 7y = 31, solid, through (0, 4.43) and (7, 1.43). The unwanted side of each line, away from the origin, is shaded. R is the clear region with vertices (0, 0), (5, 0), (3.8, 2.8) and (0, 31/7), about (0, 4.43). Whole-number points of R, shown as dots: (0, 0) to (0, 4); (1, 0) to (1, 4); (2, 0) to (2, 3); (3, 0) to (3, 3); (4, 0) to (4, 2); and (5, 0). The vertex (3.8, 2.8) is marked with a ring and the point (4, 3), outside R, with a cross. Key: shaded, not wanted; unshaded (R), the required region.</p></description>
                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[width=9cm, height=8cm, xmin=0, xmax=7, ymin=0, ymax=6,
  axis lines=left, xtick={0,1,...,7}, ytick={0,1,...,6}, grid=major, grid style={gray!35},
  xlabel={$x$ (beehives)}, ylabel={$y$ (cages)}, clip=true]
\fill[gray!45, opacity=0.6] (axis cs:5,0) -- (axis cs:7,0) -- (axis cs:7,6) -- (axis cs:17/7,6) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!45, opacity=0.6] (axis cs:0,31/7) -- (axis cs:0,6) -- (axis cs:7,6) -- (axis cs:7,10/7) -- cycle;
\addplot[thick, domain=17/7:5, samples=2] {(35-7*x)/3};
\addplot[thick, domain=0:7, samples=2] {(31-3*x)/7};
\addplot[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.6pt] coordinates {(0,0) (0,1) (0,2) (0,3) (0,4) (1,0) (1,1) (1,2) (1,3) (1,4) (2,0) (2,1) (2,2) (2,3) (3,0) (3,1) (3,2) (3,3) (4,0) (4,1) (4,2) (5,0)};
\addplot[only marks, mark=o, mark size=3pt, thick] coordinates {(3.8,2.8)};
\addplot[only marks, mark=x, mark size=4pt, very thick] coordinates {(4,3)};
\node[font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt, anchor=north west] at (axis cs:4.05,1.95) {$(4, 2)$};
\node[font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt, anchor=south west] at (axis cs:4.1,3.05) {$(4, 3)$};
\node[font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt, anchor=west] at (axis cs:3.95,2.8) {$(3.8, 2.8)$};
\node[font=\large] at (axis cs:1.5,1.5) {$R$};
\node[font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt, rotate=-66] at (axis cs:3.35,4.4) {$7x + 3y = 35$};
\node[font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt, rotate=-17] at (axis cs:5.8,2.2) {$3x + 7y = 31$};
\end{axis}
\node[font=\small, anchor=north] at (3.6,-1.1) {Shaded, not wanted; unshaded ($R$), the required region.};
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <note xml:id="note-sampling-in-a-spreadsheet">
            <title>Sampling in a Spreadsheet</title>
            <p>A spreadsheet can do the sampling for you. Put the values of <m>x</m> in column A and of <m>y</m> in column B, one plan per row. In column C use <c>IF</c> to test every constraint, for example <c>=IF(AND(7*A2+3*B2&lt;=35, 3*A2+7*B2&lt;=31), "in R", "out")</c>, and in column D work out the profit, <c>=900*A2+700*B2</c>. Sort the rows that are in R by profit: the top row is the best plan you sampled.</p>
        </note>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type whole numbers or exact values, for example <c>4.5</c> or <c>13/2</c>.</p>

