        <exercises xml:id="exercises-optimum-by-sampling">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-sample-r4">
                <statement><p>R is the region <m>8x + 7y \le 112</m>, <m>2x + 7y \le 70</m>, <m>x \ge 0</m>, <m>y \ge 0</m> (the region of <xref ref="ex-graph-region-r4"/>). Find the vertices of R and the maximum value of <m>P = 2x + 5y</m> on R.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Maximum <m>54</m> at <m>(7, 8)</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Subtracting <m>2x + 7y = 70</m> from <m>8x + 7y = 112</m> gives <m>6x = 42</m>, so <m>x = 7</m> and <m>y = 8</m>. The vertices are <m>(0, 0)</m>, <m>(14, 0)</m>, <m>(7, 8)</m> and <m>(0, 10)</m>, where <m>P = 0</m>, <m>28</m>, <m>54</m> and <m>50</m>. The maximum is <m>54</m> at <m>(7, 8)</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-sample-minimum">
                <statement><p>Find the minimum value of <m>C = 5x + 2y</m> subject to <m>5x + 7y \ge 70</m>, <m>9x + 2y \ge 73</m>, <m>x \ge 0</m>, <m>y \ge 0</m>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Minimum <m>45</m> at <m>(7, 5)</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Solve <m>5x + 7y = 70</m> and <m>9x + 2y = 73</m>: multiply the first by <m>2</m> and the second by <m>7</m>, then subtract: <m>53x = 371</m>, so <m>x = 7</m> and <m>y = 5</m>. The other vertices are <m>(14, 0)</m> on <m>5x + 7y = 70</m> and <m>(0, 36.5)</m> on <m>9x + 2y = 73</m>. <m>C = 70</m> at <m>(14, 0)</m>, <m>45</m> at <m>(7, 5)</m> and <m>73</m> at <m>(0, 36.5)</m>. The minimum is <m>45</m> at <m>(7, 5)</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-sample-jua-kali">
                <statement><p>Solve the jua kali model of <xref ref="example-metal-boxes-and-jikos"/>: <m>6x + y \le 96</m>, <m>x + y \le 26</m>, <m>x \ge 0</m>, <m>y \ge 0</m>, with profit <m>P = 450x + 250y</m> shillings for <m>x</m> metal boxes and <m>y</m> jikos. How many of each should the workshop make?</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>14</m> metal boxes and <m>12</m> jikos, Ksh <m>9300</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Subtracting <m>x + y = 26</m> from <m>6x + y = 96</m> gives <m>5x = 70</m>, so <m>x = 14</m> and <m>y = 12</m>. The vertices are <m>(0, 0)</m>, <m>(16, 0)</m>, <m>(14, 12)</m> and <m>(0, 26)</m>, with <m>P = 0</m>, <m>7200</m>, <m>9300</m> and <m>6500</m>. The best plan is <m>14</m> boxes and <m>12</m> jikos, a profit of Ksh <m>9300</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-sample-whole-numbers">
                <statement><p>Find the whole numbers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> that make <m>P = 3x + 8y</m> as large as possible, subject to <m>6x + 5y \le 41</m>, <m>2x + 9y \le 32</m>, <m>x \ge 0</m>, <m>y \ge 0</m>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>(5, 2)</m>, <m>P = 31</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>The boundaries meet where <m>6x + 5y = 41</m> and <m>2x + 9y = 32</m>: multiply the second by <m>3</m> and subtract the first, <m>22y = 55</m>, so <m>y = 2.5</m> and <m>x = 4.75</m>. The vertices are <m>(0, 0)</m>, <m>\left(\tfrac{41}{6}, 0\right)</m>, <m>(4.75, 2.5)</m> and <m>\left(0, \tfrac{32}{9}\right)</m>, with <m>P = 0</m>, <m>20.5</m>, <m>34.25</m> and about <m>28.44</m>. The best vertex is not a whole-number point. The whole-number points of R with <m>x = 3</m> to <m>6</m> and <m>y = 1</m> to <m>4</m> are <m>(3, 1)</m>, <m>(3, 2)</m>, <m>(4, 1)</m>, <m>(4, 2)</m>, <m>(5, 1)</m>, <m>(5, 2)</m> and <m>(6, 1)</m>, with <m>P = 17, 25, 20, 28, 23, 31, 26</m>. The best is <m>(5, 2)</m>: it uses <m>40 \le 41</m> and <m>28 \le 32</m>, and <m>P = 31</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-sample-tie">
                <statement><p>Find the maximum value of <m>P = 2x + 7y</m> subject to <m>6x + y \le 48</m>, <m>2x + 7y \le 56</m>, <m>x \ge 0</m>, <m>y \ge 0</m>. Which points give it? Which of them are whole-number points?</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>56</m>, at every point of the edge from <m>(7, 6)</m> to <m>(0, 8)</m>; whole-number points <m>(7, 6)</m> and <m>(0, 8)</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>The vertices are <m>(0, 0)</m>, <m>(8, 0)</m>, <m>(7, 6)</m> and <m>(0, 8)</m>, with <m>P = 0</m>, <m>16</m>, <m>56</m> and <m>56</m>. Two vertices tie. The objective <m>2x + 7y</m> is the left-hand side of the boundary <m>2x + 7y = 56</m>, so every point of the edge joining <m>(7, 6)</m> and <m>(0, 8)</m> gives <m>P = 56</m>. On that edge <m>y = (56 - 2x) \div 7</m> is a whole number only when <m>x</m> is a multiple of <m>7</m>, so the whole-number optima are <m>(7, 6)</m> and <m>(0, 8)</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-which-profit-keeps-the-corner">
                <title>Challenge: Which Profit Keeps the Corner?</title>
                <statement><p>On the region of <xref ref="ex-sample-tie"/>, the objective is changed to <m>P = kx + y</m>, where <m>k \gt 0</m>. For which values of <m>k</m> is <m>(7, 6)</m> an optimal solution?</p></statement>
                <hint><p>Compare the values of <m>P</m> at <m>(8, 0)</m>, <m>(7, 6)</m> and <m>(0, 8)</m>: <m>8k</m>, <m>7k + 6</m> and <m>8</m>.</p></hint>
                <answer><p><m>\tfrac{2}{7} \le k \le 6</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>(7, 6)</m> is optimal when <m>7k + 6 \ge 8k</m> and <m>7k + 6 \ge 8</m> (the value at <m>(0, 0)</m> is <m>0</m>). The first gives <m>k \le 6</m> and the second <m>k \ge \tfrac{2}{7}</m>. So <m>\tfrac{2}{7} \le k \le 6</m>. At either end two vertices tie.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-optimum-by-sampling">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Give a sampling table template with columns <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, one column per constraint (<q>holds?</q>) and <m>P</m>. Work through <xref ref="example-tailor-best-week"/> and <xref ref="example-cheapest-fertiliser"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-sample-r4"/> and <xref ref="ex-sample-jua-kali"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-sample-r4"/> to <xref ref="ex-sample-tie"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell><p>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-which-profit-keeps-the-corner"/>.</p><p component="core">Extension: draw the line <m>2x + 5y = 20</m> on the region of <xref ref="ex-sample-r4"/> and slide a ruler parallel to it, away from the origin. Where does it leave R? (Answer: at <m>(7, 8)</m>, the optimum found by sampling.)</p><p component="essential">Extension: on the region of <xref ref="ex-sample-r4"/>, test every whole-number point with <m>y = 8</m> in <m>P = 2x + 5y</m>. Which is best? (Answer: <m>(0, 8)</m> to <m>(7, 8)</m> are in R and <m>(7, 8)</m> gives the largest value, <m>54</m>.)</p></cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 3.2 (Core only) -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-the-search-line" component="core">
        <title>The Search Line</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> all plans with the same value of <m>P = ax + by</m> lie on one line; these lines are parallel, with gradient <m>-\tfrac{a}{b}</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> draw a search line and slide it, away from the origin for a maximum and towards it for a minimum, to find the optimal vertex or whole-number point; confirm a vertex by comparing gradients.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> check the sampling answers for the tailor, the fertiliser and the youth group.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners see that plans with equal profit lie on parallel lines, and use a ruler to find the last point of R such a line touches.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Give each group a ruler and a set square and the graph of the fundraiser region from <xref ref="act-graphing-the-fundraiser"/>. After part (b), ask: <q>What do all these lines have in common?</q></p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-lines-of-equal-profit">
            <title>Lines of Equal Profit</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Use your graph of the fundraiser region R and the profit <m>P = 15x + 25y</m> shillings from <xref ref="act-which-plan-earns-most"/>.</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Show that the plans <m>(100, 0)</m>, <m>(0, 60)</m> and <m>(50, 30)</m> each earn Ksh <m>1500</m>. Plot them. What do you notice?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>15 \times 100 = 1500</m>, <m>25 \times 60 = 1500</m>, <m>750 + 750 = 1500</m>. The three points lie on one straight line, <m>15x + 25y = 1500</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Draw the lines of plans earning Ksh <m>1800</m> and Ksh <m>2100</m>. How are the three lines related?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>15x + 25y = 1800</m> passes through <m>(120, 0)</m> and <m>(0, 72)</m>; <m>15x + 25y = 2100</m> through <m>(140, 0)</m> and <m>(0, 84)</m>. All three lines are parallel, with gradient <m>-\tfrac{15}{25} = -\tfrac{3}{5}</m>; a bigger profit gives a line further from the origin.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Lay your ruler along the Ksh <m>1500</m> line and slide it parallel to itself, away from the origin. Which point of R does it touch last? What is the profit there?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>The corner <m>(49, 51)</m>, with <m>P = 735 + 1275 = 2010</m>: the same answer as sampling gave.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Does the Ksh <m>2100</m> line meet R? What does that tell the class?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>No: it lies beyond <m>(49, 51)</m>. No plan in R earns Ksh <m>2100</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To name the search line and state the method for a maximum, a minimum and a whole-number answer.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> the lines of part (b) are search lines: one profit, one line.</p></li>
                    <li><p>For <m>P = ax + by</m> every search line has gradient <m>-\tfrac{a}{b}</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Slide away from the origin for a maximum, towards it for a minimum. For whole numbers, stop at the last whole-number point of R.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The gradient check confirms the vertex without drawing.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-search-line">
            <title>The Search Line</title>
            <p>For the objective function <m>P = ax + by</m>, all the plans with <m>P = k</m> lie on the line <m>ax + by = k</m>. This line is called a <term>search line</term>, or a line of equal profit (a line of equal cost when the objective is a cost). Changing <m>k</m> moves the line without turning it: all search lines are parallel, with gradient <m>-\tfrac{a}{b}</m>. Every point on one search line gives the same value, so slide it until it is about to leave R.</p>
            <p>To find the optimal solution with a search line, when <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are positive:
                <ol marker="1.">
                    <li><p>Draw one search line <m>ax + by = k</m> for a convenient <m>k</m> (a multiple of both <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> gives whole-number intercepts).</p></li>
                    <li><p>Slide a ruler parallel to it: away from the origin for a maximum, towards the origin for a minimum.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The last point of R that the ruler touches is the optimal solution. Find its exact coordinates by solving the equations of the two boundaries that meet there.</p></li>
                    <li><p>If the answer must be whole numbers, the optimal solution is the last whole-number point of R that the ruler touches.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
            <p>A gradient check: a vertex is optimal when the gradient of the search line lies between the gradients of the two edges of R that meet at that vertex.</p>
        </insight>

        <figure xml:id="fig-search-lines-fundraiser">
            <caption>Three search lines for <m>P = 15x + 25y</m> on the fundraiser region. The line <m>P = 2010</m> touches R only at <m>(49, 51)</m>.</caption>
            <image width="90%">
                <shortdescription>The fundraiser region R with solid boundaries x + y = 100, 15x + 35y = 2520 and y = 30, the unwanted sides shaded. Three parallel dashed search lines 15x + 25y = 1500, 1800 and 2010 cross the graph; the line P = 2010 touches R only at the corner (49, 51).</shortdescription>
                <description><p>Horizontal axis x, mandazi, from 0 to 110. Vertical axis y, samosas, from 0 to 110. Boundaries, all solid: x + y = 100 through (0, 100) and (100, 0); 15x + 35y = 2520 through (0, 72) and (168, 0); y = 30. The side of each boundary that is not wanted is shaded. R, left clear, has vertices (0, 30), (70, 30), (49, 51) and (0, 72). Dashed search lines: 15x + 25y = 1500 through (0, 60) and (100, 0), crossing R; 15x + 25y = 1800 through (0, 72) and (120, 0), crossing R; 15x + 25y = 2010 through (0, 80.4) and (134, 0), meeting R only at (49, 51). Key: shaded, not wanted; unshaded (R), the required region.</p></description>
                <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[width=12cm, height=10cm, xmin=0, xmax=110, ymin=0, ymax=110,
  axis lines=left, xtick={0,10,...,110}, ytick={0,10,...,110}, grid=major, grid style={gray!35},
  xlabel={$x$ (mandazi)}, ylabel={$y$ (samosas)}, clip=true]
\fill[gray!45, opacity=0.5] (axis cs:0,100) -- (axis cs:0,110) -- (axis cs:110,110) -- (axis cs:110,0) -- (axis cs:100,0) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!45, opacity=0.5] (axis cs:0,72) -- (axis cs:0,110) -- (axis cs:110,110) -- (axis cs:110,870/35) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!45, opacity=0.5] (axis cs:0,0) -- (axis cs:110,0) -- (axis cs:110,30) -- (axis cs:0,30) -- cycle;
\addplot[thick, domain=0:100, samples=2] {100-x};
\addplot[thick, domain=0:110, samples=2] {(2520-15*x)/35};
\addplot[thick, domain=0:110, samples=2] {30};
\addplot[very thick, dashed, domain=0:100, samples=2] {(1500-15*x)/25};
\addplot[very thick, dashed, domain=0:110, samples=2] {(1800-15*x)/25};
\addplot[very thick, dashed, domain=0:110, samples=2] {(2010-15*x)/25};
\addplot[only marks, mark=*, mark size=2pt] coordinates {(0,30) (70,30) (49,51) (0,72)};
\node[font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt, anchor=south west] at (axis cs:50,52) {$(49, 51)$};
\node[font=\large] at (axis cs:25,45) {$R$};
\node[font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:85,9) {$P = 1500$};
\node[font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:96,15) {$P = 1800$};
\node[font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] at (axis cs:100,26) {$P = 2010$};
\node[font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt, anchor=west] at (axis cs:2,104) {$x + y = 100$};
\node[font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt, anchor=west] at (axis cs:80,40) {$15x + 35y = 2520$};
\node[font=\small, fill=white, inner sep=1pt, anchor=west] at (axis cs:75,33.5) {$y = 30$};
\end{axis}
\node[font=\small, anchor=north] at (5.3,-1.1) {Shaded, not wanted; unshaded ($R$), the required region. Dashed lines: search lines.};
\end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <figure xml:id="fig-lp-search-line">
            <caption>Drag the slider to move the search line away from the origin. Watch where it last touches the region.</caption>
            <interactive xml:id="interactive-lp-search-line" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="16:12" source="jsxgraph/lp-search-line.js">
                <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-lp-search-line" surface="jsxboard" aspect="16:12"/>
                <instructions>
                    <p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><p>The region is the tailor's, from <xref ref="act-the-tailors-week"/>: <m>3x + 7y \le 126</m>, <m>x + 6y \le 64</m>, <m>x \ge 0</m>, <m>y \ge 0</m>. The dashed line is the search line <m>300x + 800y = k</m>.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Drag the <alert>k</alert> slider from <m>0</m> to <m>22\,000</m>. While the line crosses R, every point of R on it earns <m>P = k</m>. When the line leaves R it turns grey and the text names the last point it touched: <m>(28, 6)</m>, with <m>P = 13\,200</m>.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Press <alert>Change the profit</alert> to use <m>P = 500x + 300y</m> instead. Where does the line leave R now?</p></li>
                            <li><p>Tick <alert>Whole numbers only</alert> to show the whole-number points of R. The text reports the last one the line passes.</p></li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </instructions>
            </interactive>
        </figure>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-search-line-tailor">
            <title>Search Line for the Tailor</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Use a search line to find the tailor's best plan for <m>P = 300x + 800y</m> on the region <m>3x + 7y \le 126</m>, <m>x + 6y \le 64</m>, <m>x \ge 0</m>, <m>y \ge 0</m>. Check your answer with gradients.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Take <m>k = 2400</m>, a multiple of both <m>300</m> and <m>800</m>. The search line <m>300x + 800y = 2400</m> passes through <m>(8, 0)</m> and <m>(0, 3)</m>. Slide a ruler parallel to it, away from the origin. The last point of R it touches is the vertex where <m>3x + 7y = 126</m> and <m>x + 6y = 64</m> meet, <m>(28, 6)</m>. There <m>P = 13\,200</m>.</p>
                <p>Gradient check: the search line has gradient <m>-\tfrac{300}{800} = -\tfrac{3}{8} = -0.375</m>. The cloth edge <m>3x + 7y = 126</m> has gradient <m>-\tfrac{3}{7} \approx -0.43</m> and the machine edge <m>x + 6y = 64</m> has gradient <m>-\tfrac{1}{6} \approx -0.17</m>. Since <m>-0.375</m> lies between them, <m>(28, 6)</m> is optimal: <m>28</m> shirts and <m>6</m> dresses, Ksh <m>13\,200</m>, as sampling found in <xref ref="example-tailor-best-week"/>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-search-line-minimum">
            <title>Search Line Towards the Origin</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Use a search line to find the cheapest fertiliser plan of <xref ref="example-cheapest-fertiliser"/>: minimise <m>C = 4000x + 7000y</m> subject to <m>x + y \ge 7</m>, <m>x + 6y \ge 17</m>, <m>x \ge 0</m>, <m>y \ge 0</m>.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Draw the line of equal cost <m>4000x + 7000y = 56\,000</m> through <m>(14, 0)</m> and <m>(0, 8)</m>; it crosses R. For a minimum, slide it towards the origin. The last point of R it touches is the vertex <m>(5, 2)</m>, where <m>x + y = 7</m> meets <m>x + 6y = 17</m>.</p>
                <p>Check: the search line's gradient <m>-\tfrac{4}{7} \approx -0.57</m> lies between <m>-1</m> (for <m>x + y = 7</m>) and <m>-\tfrac{1}{6}</m> (for <m>x + 6y = 17</m>). The minimum cost is <m>C = 20\,000 + 14\,000 = 34\,000</m>: <m>5</m> bags of A and <m>2</m> of B, Ksh <m>34\,000</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-search-line-whole-numbers">
            <title>Search Line and Whole Numbers</title>
            <statement>
                <p>In <xref ref="example-beehives-and-cages"/> the best vertex <m>(3.8, 2.8)</m> is not a whole-number point. Use a search line for <m>P = 900x + 700y</m> to find the best whole-number plan.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>The search lines <m>900x + 700y = k</m> have gradient <m>-\tfrac{9}{7}</m>. The line through <m>(3.8, 2.8)</m> has <m>k = 5380</m> and touches R only at that vertex. Slide the line back towards the origin and watch for the first whole-number points of R it meets (see the dots in <xref ref="fig-beehives-region"/>):
                    <md>(4, 2)\colon P = 5000, \quad (3, 3)\colon P = 4800, \quad (5, 0)\colon P = 4500</md>.
                    The first one met is <m>(4, 2)</m>. Moving outwards from the origin, it is the last whole-number point of R the line touches, so it is the best whole-number plan: <m>4</m> beehives and <m>2</m> cages, Ksh <m>5000</m>. This agrees with sampling.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type whole numbers or exact values, for example <c>4.5</c> or <c>13/2</c>.</p>

                <exercise xml:id="ex-the-search-line" component="core">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-the-search-line" source="stack/top/1-numbers-and-algebra/05-linear-programming/03-optimisation-of-linear-inequalities/The-search-line.xml"/>
        </exercise>

                <exercise xml:id="ex-which-vertex-is-optimal" component="core">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-which-vertex-is-optimal" source="stack/top/1-numbers-and-algebra/05-linear-programming/03-optimisation-of-linear-inequalities/Which-vertex-is-optimal.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-search-line">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-search-line-r5">
                <statement><p>R is the region <m>7x + 4y \le 84</m>, <m>x + 8y \le 64</m>, <m>x \ge 0</m>, <m>y \ge 0</m> (<xref ref="ex-vertices-r5"/>). Use a search line to find the maximum of <m>P = 5x + 7y</m> on R.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>89</m> at <m>(8, 7)</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Draw <m>5x + 7y = 35</m> through <m>(7, 0)</m> and <m>(0, 5)</m> and slide it away from the origin. It leaves R at the vertex where <m>7x + 4y = 84</m> and <m>x + 8y = 64</m> meet: <m>(8, 7)</m>. Gradient check: <m>-\tfrac{5}{7}</m> lies between <m>-\tfrac{7}{4}</m> and <m>-\tfrac{1}{8}</m>. <m>P = 40 + 49 = 89</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-search-line-minimum">
                <statement><p>Use a search line to find the minimum of <m>C = 11x + 7y</m> subject to <m>7x + 2y \ge 29</m>, <m>x + 6y \ge 27</m>, <m>x \ge 0</m>, <m>y \ge 0</m>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>61</m> at <m>(3, 4)</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Draw <m>11x + 7y = 77</m> through <m>(7, 0)</m> and <m>(0, 11)</m> and slide it towards the origin. It leaves R at the vertex where <m>7x + 2y = 29</m> and <m>x + 6y = 27</m> meet. From the second, <m>x = 27 - 6y</m>; then <m>189 - 40y = 29</m>, so <m>y = 4</m> and <m>x = 3</m>. Gradient check: <m>-\tfrac{11}{7}</m> lies between <m>-\tfrac{7}{2}</m> and <m>-\tfrac{1}{6}</m>. <m>C = 33 + 28 = 61</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-change-the-profit">
                <statement><p>The tailor's profits change to Ksh <m>500</m> a shirt and Ksh <m>300</m> a dress, so <m>P = 500x + 300y</m> on the same region (<xref ref="example-search-line-tailor"/>). Use a search line to find the new best plan.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>42</m> shirts and no dresses, Ksh <m>21\,000</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>The search lines have gradient <m>-\tfrac{5}{3}</m>, steeper than the cloth edge (gradient <m>-\tfrac{3}{7}</m>). Draw <m>500x + 300y = 1500</m> through <m>(3, 0)</m> and <m>(0, 5)</m> and slide it away from the origin. The last point of R it touches is <m>(42, 0)</m>, where the cloth edge meets the <m>x</m>-axis. <m>P = 21\,000</m> there, compared with <m>15\,800</m> at <m>(28, 6)</m>: <m>42</m> shirts and no dresses.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-search-line-along-an-edge">
                <statement><p>In <xref ref="ex-sample-tie"/>, the maximum of <m>P = 2x + 7y</m> is reached all along an edge of R. Explain this using search lines.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>The search lines <m>2x + 7y = k</m> are parallel to the edge <m>2x + 7y = 56</m>.</p></answer>
                <solution><p>Every search line <m>2x + 7y = k</m> is parallel to the boundary <m>2x + 7y = 56</m>. Sliding away from the origin, the last search line that meets R is <m>2x + 7y = 56</m> itself, and it touches R along the whole edge from <m>(7, 6)</m> to <m>(0, 8)</m>, not at a single point.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-profit-range-tailor">
                <title>Challenge: How Far Can the Shirt Profit Move?</title>
                <statement><p>The tailor's profit is <m>P = kx + 800y</m> shillings on the region of <xref ref="example-search-line-tailor"/>. For which values of <m>k</m> is <m>(28, 6)</m> still an optimal solution?</p></statement>
                <hint><p>Use the gradient check: <m>-\tfrac{k}{800}</m> must lie between the gradients of the two edges that meet at <m>(28, 6)</m>.</p></hint>
                <answer><p><m>\tfrac{400}{3} \le k \le \tfrac{2400}{7}</m>, that is about <m>133.33 \le k \le 342.86</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>The edges at <m>(28, 6)</m> have gradients <m>-\tfrac{3}{7}</m> and <m>-\tfrac{1}{6}</m>. We need <m>\tfrac{1}{6} \le \tfrac{k}{800} \le \tfrac{3}{7}</m>, so <m>\tfrac{800}{6} \le k \le \tfrac{2400}{7}</m>, that is <m>\tfrac{400}{3} \le k \le \tfrac{2400}{7}</m> (about <m>133.33</m> to <m>342.86</m>). At either end the search line lies along an edge and two vertices tie.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-the-search-line">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Give a card showing how to slide a ruler along a set square, and pre-drawn regions. Work through <xref ref="example-search-line-tailor"/> and <xref ref="example-search-line-minimum"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-search-line-r5"/> and <xref ref="ex-change-the-profit"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-search-line-r5"/> to <xref ref="ex-search-line-along-an-edge"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-profit-range-tailor"/>. Extension: for <m>P = 300x + by</m> on the tailor's region, which values of <m>b</m> keep <m>(28, 6)</m> optimal? (Answer: <m>700 \le b \le 1800</m>, since <m>\tfrac{300}{b}</m> must lie between <m>\tfrac{1}{6}</m> and <m>\tfrac{3}{7}</m>.)</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>
    </subsection>
